  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSynoptically for the Long Term period, on Saturday there is a ridge \nthat will begin to transition to more zonal flow for the rest of the \nperiod. Throughout the week there will be some embedded shortwave \ndisturbances moving through that will create some precipitation \nchances for the County Warning Area (CWA).\n\nOverall for the Long Term forecast, it is looking to be hot, dry, \nand windy. High temperatures for this period are forecast to be in \nthe high 90s, with Monday potentially reaching the low 100s. Winds \nlook to be breezy to gusty and mainly form the south to east for the \nmajority of the CWA as shortwaves move through the region.\n\nMonday does have some fire weather concerns. Monday's highs are \nforecast to be in the high 90s to low 100s for the majority of the \nCWA. Relative humidity values are looking to be in the high teens to \nlow 20s, particularly for areas west of KS Hwy 27. Looking at the \nwind gusts, they are currently forecast to be in the range of 20-40 \nmph for the majority of the CWA. Moving to GFDI, values mainly for \nEastern Colorado are in the 30-59, which would promote very high \nfire growth. Another concern is the threat of dry lightning from any \nthunderstorms that develop over Eastern Colorado. With these \nconditions elevated fire weather is possible; however there are some \ndiscrepancies regarding the timing of when all the ingredients for \nelevated criteria will be met at the same time.",
